 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ for single-axis rotation as it involves a fixed number of trigonometric operations and assignments. $O(N)$ for batched vector-to-vector rotation where $N$ is the batch size.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for single matrix construction. $O(N)$ for batched operations.
-- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Scaling:** Linear with batch size for vector-to-vector method. Constant for axis-angle.
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement for standard axes. Euler angles allow intuitive composition of rotations. Vector-to-vector method avoids gimbal lock for specific alignments.
-- **Limitations:** Euler angles suffer from gimbal lock. Vector-to-vector method has numerical instability when vectors are parallel (handled by edge cases). Trigonometric functions are computationally expensive compared to linear algebra operations.
-- **When to use:** Use single-axis for simple rotations around world axes. Use Euler angles for camera orientation control. Use vector-to-vector for aligning view directions.
-- **When not to use:** Avoid Euler angles for continuous rotation interpolation (use quaternions). Avoid vector-to-vector if performance is critical and rotations are pre-defined.
